1) useProject.ts compute(): leave the snapshot `r` as it is for building the worker input. After the last await before the state commit (i.e. after canvasToJpeg for the thumb), merge the result into the latest record instead of the snapshot:
   const latest = recRef.current ?? r;
   const resultKeyBase = r.settings.paletteMode === 'custom' ? { ...r, palette: res.palette } : r;
   const next = { ...latest, palette: res.palette, diagnostics: res.diagnostics, computedWith: computeKey(resultKeyBase), meta: { ...latest.meta, computed: true, thumb, updatedAt: Date.now() } };
   recRef.current = next; setRec(next); ... await saveProject(next) (as now).
   computedWith must stay the key of the inputs that were actually computed (the snapshot, plus the renumbered palette in custom mode), never computeKey(latest). Then name, PDF options, grout depth and reserve survive, and changes to joint, size, adjust or regions made during the compute show up as the existing stale badge. Do not move side effects into a setRec updater; recRef.current is already updated synchronously by update().
2) Workspace.tsx:84: change onDone to `() => setStep((cur) => (cur === 'compute' ? 'preview' : cur))`. Optionally show a toast or badge "Berechnung fertig – Vorschau öffnen" when the user is on another step. Leave-the-workspace-during-compute is a separate issue.
3) StepColors: while api.computing is set, disable only palette editing (add/remove/edit colours), with a short note. The finished result must overwrite the palette because the mosaic's colour indices refer to res.palette, so palette edits made during a compute would be lost anyway. Keep all other inputs enabled, since the merge in step 1 handles them.
